  "worked_solution": "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
